cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use SE-MPPI in your research, please cite it as below."
title: "SE-MPPI: a Nav2 controller plugin integrating local-minima escape for MPPI with a control-barrier-function safety filter"
authors:
  - family-names: "Kang"
    given-names: "Jungmo"
    email: "kangjmo91@gmail.com"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0002-0394-1861"
repository-code: "https://github.com/kjungmo/se-mppi-nav2"
license: Apache-2.0
date-released: "2026-10-22"
version: "2.0.0-pre"
abstract: >-
  The MPPI controller in ROS 2 Nav2 can stall in local minima in crowded
  dynamic spaces, and its critics avoid collisions only through soft costs. We
  know of no public Nav2 plugin combining local-minima escape with a barrier-
  based safety filter. This system paper presents SE-MPPI, a Nav2 controller
  plugin integrating local-minima escape with a control-barrier-function (CBF)
  safety filter for tracked dynamic obstacles. A first version reached the
  goal in none of its 44 valid live trials on a correct build; we report the
  four defects identified as the cause (one inferred) and the repaired
  controller. Its escape carries four additions to the advised design: two
  were needed for the development sweeps of a pocket family to pass (ablated
  jointly, confounded with sweep position), and two are safeguards. In a pre-
  registered live campaign (one post hoc amendment to its crash-stop rule),
  SE-MPPI without recoveries reached the goal in 5 of 6 trials on s1 and 5 of
  6 on the held-out s4; stock MPPI reached it in 0 of 6 on each (Fisher
  p=0.0152 each). With Nav2's default recovery tree on s1: stock 0 of 6, SE-
  MPPI 3 of 6 (p=0.1818, descriptive). On s3 (near mirror twin in the
  development family): 4 of 6 against 0 of 6 (p=0.0606, not significant). The
  claim stops at one simulated host, six repetitions per cell, no real robot.
  In a randomized, paired 1,200-trial benchmark on a Python 2D mirror, not on
  the Nav2 controller itself, escape raises U-trap success from 0% to 80%
  (Wilson 95% interval [67,89]); a second arm of 1,200 trials on the same 200
  seeded worlds, with the four additions off, reaches 58%. Escape-safety
  coordination matches an independent escape+CBF stack, so we claim it only as
  a mechanism. The gain schedule preserves CBF forward invariance only
  conditionally: for a look-ahead point and tracked dynamic obstacles, if the
  barrier inequality holds between updates and the QP is slack-free.
keywords:
  - ROS 2
  - Nav2
  - MPPI
  - control barrier functions
  - local planner
  - collision avoidance
preferred-citation:
  type: unpublished
  title: "SE-MPPI: Coordinating Online Local-Minima Escape with Control-Barrier-Function Safety in a Nav2-Native Controller"
  authors:
